WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.313
The Leaning Tower of Pisa is one of the architectural wonders of the world, and permanently fascinating to people.

2
00:00:07.314 --> 00:00:11.600
The famous bell tower has stood for over six centuries.

3
00:00:11.601 --> 00:00:20.441
But it hasn't ever stood straight—at one point in history, the tower tilted about 4.5 meters from the vertical!

4
00:00:20.442 --> 00:00:23.910
Pisa is an ancient city in Tuscany, Italy.

5
00:00:23.911 --> 00:00:31.353
Located on the river Arno, with easy access to the sea, the city became a major port of trade.

6
00:00:31.354 --> 00:00:41.034
In the eleventh century, merchants from across the Mediterranean and as far away as the Middle East came to buy and sell goods.

7
00:00:41.035 --> 00:00:48.349
The city's inhabitants, the Pisans, often had to defend their city against outside forces.

8
00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:57.795
They also made their own conquests. In 1063 they sailed to the island of Sicily and attacked the city of Palermo.

9
00:00:57.796 --> 00:01:02.847
The defeat of Palermo made the Pisans rich and confident in their power.

10
00:01:02.848 --> 00:01:07.424
They decided to build a large cathedral to show off their treasures.

11
00:01:07.425 --> 00:01:14.385
A large piece of open land sat in the middle of the city, which the Pisans called the Square of Miracles.

12
00:01:14.386 --> 00:01:22.042
They decided to build the cathedral on the square, as a tribute to Pisa's conquests and rich heritage.

13
00:01:22.043 --> 00:01:27.892
The front of the building was covered with stones stolen from ancient Roman ruins.

14
00:01:27.893 --> 00:01:37.612
Even today you can read ancient Roman inscriptions, some of which are upside down or sideways, in the marble stones of the cathedral.

15
00:01:37.613 --> 00:01:43.749
The building took a very long time to complete, almost three hundred years.

16
00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:49.403
The Pisans also decided to build a cemetery and a tall bell tower in the square.

17
00:01:49.404 --> 00:01:57.286
Construction of the bell tower, which would later become known as the Leaning Tower of Pisa, began in 1173.

18
00:01:57.287 --> 00:02:06.518
Not long after the workers had laid the foundation and built the first three floors, they noticed that the building was sinking on the south side.

19
00:02:06.519 --> 00:02:12.649
This was not surprising because the soil underneath the Square of Miracles was very marshy.

20
00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:16.057
It was a common problem for buildings in the area.

21
00:02:16.058 --> 00:02:20.924
In fact, the name Pisa means "marshy land" in Greek.

22
00:02:20.925 --> 00:02:27.641
Around this time war broke out, and construction on the bell tower stopped for nearly one hundred years.

23
00:02:27.642 --> 00:02:32.142
This long interruption turned out to be good for the tilting tower.

24
00:02:32.143 --> 00:02:39.561
It gave the building's foundation time to settle, and probably even prevented it from collapsing long ago.

25
00:02:39.562 --> 00:02:49.471
Experts believe that when construction on the tower restarted, an engineer named Bonanno Pisano was in charge of the challenging project.

26
00:02:49.472 --> 00:02:57.848
In order to compensate for the building's tilt, Pisano decided to make the columns and arches on the south side a little longer.

27
00:02:57.849 --> 00:03:00.585
He believed that this would level out the tower.

28
00:03:00.586 --> 00:03:04.000
But Pisano's solution only worsened the problem.

29
00:03:04.001 --> 00:03:07.902
The extra weight made the tower sink some more!

30
00:03:07.903 --> 00:03:14.977
As years passed, other engineers and builders took on the challenge of finishing the building, and fixing its tilt.

31
00:03:14.978 --> 00:03:23.629
By 1350 the tower's bell chamber had been constructed, and the rest of the tower was finally completed around 1372.

32
00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:29.790
When the tower was finished, many people were glad that all the builders had not quit the project.

33
00:03:29.791 --> 00:03:36.665
It was truly an architectural wonder, with beautiful arches and two hundred marble columns.

34
00:03:36.666 --> 00:03:40.003
But by now the tower's tilt was very noticeable.

35
00:03:40.004 --> 00:03:46.233
The tilt fascinated people, yet it worried them too; one day the tower could collapse.

36
00:03:46.234 --> 00:03:49.842
Over time many efforts were made to save the tower.

37
00:03:49.843 --> 00:03:55.417
Some of the solutions were realistic, while others were almost comical.

38
00:03:55.418 --> 00:04:04.600
One person suggested draining all of the water beneath the foundation of the tower; another suggested leaving the water there but freezing it!

39
00:04:04.601 --> 00:04:09.320
Other ideas included moving the tower to another location or rebuilding it.

40
00:04:09.321 --> 00:04:20.511
In the 1920s experts tried injecting cement into the foundation of the tower in order to stabilize it, or at least slow the rate at which it was tilting.

41
00:04:20.512 --> 00:04:25.524
Despite all these engineering efforts, the tower continued sinking.

42
00:04:25.525 --> 00:04:30.249
By the 1960s the Italian government was so concerned about the tower,

43
00:04:30.250 --> 00:04:35.617
they announced that they were looking for suggestions to solve this puzzling problem.

44
00:04:35.618 --> 00:04:41.394
Finally, in 1990, it was decided to close the building for safety reasons.

45
00:04:41.395 --> 00:04:45.956
A major project was eventually launched to straighten the tower.

46
00:04:45.957 --> 00:04:52.581
This time engineers slowly removed earth from underneath the foundation on its north side.

47
00:04:52.582 --> 00:04:59.108
When the tower reopened in 2001, the tilt had been reduced by about 44 centimeters!

48
00:04:59.109 --> 00:05:02.528
And over time the tilt decreased even more.

49
00:05:02.529 --> 00:05:08.335
The tall tower, with its unusual tilt, has fascinated not just builders and tourists.

50
00:05:08.336 --> 00:05:16.126
Galileo Galilei, a former resident of Pisa, was born about two hundred years after the tower was finished.

51
00:05:16.127 --> 00:05:20.566
He used it to conduct many important scientific experiments.

52
00:05:20.567 --> 00:05:27.802
In one famous story, Galileo wanted to prove his theory that all objects fall at the same speed.

53
00:05:27.803 --> 00:05:36.491
So he climbed to the top of the Tower of Pisa and dropped two balls at the same time.

54
00:05:36.492 --> 00:05:43.598
According to the story, both balls hit the ground at the same time even though they differed in weight.

55
00:05:43.599 --> 00:05:49.332
Today the bell tower continues to draw tourists and admirers from around the world.

56
00:05:49.333 --> 00:05:57.606
Engineers believe, due to recent measurements, that the building will remain stable now for at least two hundred years.

57
00:05:57.607 --> 00:06:07.779
So for generations to come, people can continue to look in amazement at the Leaning Tower of Pisa.

